cartridge "ALCHEMIST'S APPRENTICE/C.C.PETERSON/1986"

' ALCHEMIST'S APPRENTICE
' A ColecoVision game based on "Alchemist's Apprentice" by Cheryl Cox
' Peterson, Ahoy! May 1986 (Commodore 64 BASIC, disk ahoy1986-05; the
' author is named on the listing's title screen). Made for Amy Studio by
' Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: the alchemist is away at his association's convention, and
' you, his apprentice, try to turn as many stones into gold as you can and
' hide it before he comes back. Each turn something happens at the
' cauldron: a spell that works (10 gold, and maybe 10 more, and more...),
' one that half works (1-6 stones, again if you dare), a counterspell
' learnt, time wasted, a hungry monster, or you turn yourself into an
' animal. After most events you may start cleaning up: that ends the game
' with the gold you have. If the alchemist walks in first, you lose it all
' and get a beating; a monster can also eat you.
'
' From the original (orig/alchemist.bas): every event and its odds (one
' roll of 1-10 each turn: 1 found it, 2 partly, 3 early return, 4 and 10
' counterspell, 5-6 wasted time, 7-8 monster, 9 animal), the chain of
' found-it spells (of 15: 1 the alchemist returns, 3 the weather spoils
' it, 11 another 10 gold), the partly-successful spell (1-6 stones; the
' same spell again: of 15, 1 early return, 2 weather, 12 the same stones
' again), the early return when 20 minutes have gone, the counterspell
' that beats any monster once learnt, the nine wasted-time events and
' their minutes, the spell book found once (and when more than 40 minutes
' have gone, the alchemist is back), the nine monsters and the counter
' rolls (of 9: 2 another one, 4 it's gone, 2 desperate then chicken, 1
' eaten), the animals (warm-blooded: swish your tail, 1 in 2; cold-
' blooded: flick your tongue until you are a goat first), the clock (on
' one start in ten the alchemist calls, warnings at 15, 30 and 45 minutes,
' back at 60), a minute for each turn once the clock runs, all the texts.
'
' Liberties: joystick menus instead of typed letters; the workshop drawn
' with the cauldron, a gold pile that grows, a window that rains when the
' weather spoils a spell, the creatures and animals as sprites, the
' alchemist at the door, sounds and a title tune. A wall clock shows the
' minutes gone (the original only printed them in messages). The calls
' come when the minutes pass 15, 30 and 45 and he is back from 60 on (the
' listing compared for exactly 15, 30, 45 and 60, so jumps of several
' minutes skipped them). The goldfish (unreachable in the listing: its
' roll stops at 12) is one of 13 animals. The friend's call costs the 10
' minutes it says (the listing added 20). The listing kept the weather in
' its roll variable Y, so a 1 or a 3 left over from a same-spell roll
' also blocked later events; here a spoilt weather flag blocks found-it
' as Y=1 meant. Gold stops at 999. A high score and a games count.
'
' Controls: either controller; the stick chooses, a button answers.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off,
' display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after; the title
' tune is played once. `set sprite count` always given. Own xorshift
' random numbers. Nothing flashes faster than 3 Hz.

